**Don't power the relay from A7.** A7 (PA7, header pin 2) is a 3.3 V logic pin that can only source a few mA to about 20 mA. A 5 V relay coil usually draws 70–90 mA, so it won't work and you risk damaging the pin. Use A7 only as the signal:

| Relay module | Flipper header |
|---|---|
| VCC | pin 1 (5 V), or an external 5 V supply |
| GND | pin 8, 11 or 18 |
| IN | pin 2 (A7) |

- **5 V pin:** it's only live while OTG 5 V output is enabled. The app has to turn it on (`furi_hal_power_enable_otg()`), or you can enable it from the GPIO app's 5V toggle. It's best to use an external supply if the coil draws a lot, with the grounds tied together.
- **3.3 V trigger:** some modules won't trigger reliably from a 3.3 V signal, and many are active-low, so IN low means the relay is on.

**Minimal FAP code** (unverified against the SDK; I haven't compiled or run it):

```c
#include <furi_hal_gpio.h>
#include <furi_hal_resources.h>

furi_hal_gpio_init(&gpio_ext_pa7, GpioModeOutputPushPull, GpioPullNo, GpioSpeedLow);
furi_hal_gpio_write(&gpio_ext_pa7, true);   // drive high (use false if the module is active-low)
// on exit:
furi_hal_gpio_write(&gpio_ext_pa7, false);
furi_hal_gpio_init(&gpio_ext_pa7, GpioModeAnalog, GpioPullNo, GpioSpeedLow);
```

